    kg:textSpan "in the United States. The training set contains about 3.1 hours of 3,696 utterances from 462 speakers after removing the SA recordings, in which only two sentences are spoken by multiple speakers. Figure 8 shows the histogram of the length of the training sequences, where the feature frames are extracted with the 10 ms period. The average length of the training sequences is 304 frames. We use the core test set with 192 utterances as the test set. The development set contains the remaining 1,152 " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1602_02410_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1602.02410_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1602.02410_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1602_02410_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "(LM) is a task central to Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Language Understanding. Models which can accurately place distributions over sentences not only encode complexities of language such as grammatical structure, but also distill a fair amount of information about the knowledge that a corpora may contain. Indeed, models that are able to assign a low probability to sentences that are grammatically correct but unlikely may help other tasks in fundamental language understanding like quest" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1602_02410_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1602.02410_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "1602.02410_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1602_02410_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "models and training recipes. Language Modeling (LM) has been a central task in NLP. The goal of LM is to learn a probability distribution over sequences of symbols pertaining to a language. Much work has been done on both parametric (e.g., log-linear models) and non-parametric approaches (e.g., count-based LMs). Count-based approaches (based on statistics of N-grams) typically add smoothing which account for unseen (yet possible) sequences, and have been quite successful. To this extent, Kneser-" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1602_02410_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
