    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1906_00733_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "that propagates both from upper to bottom layers and from previous to next time steps (see Fig. 1). In each layer, it uses different scales of previous samples to condition the lower layer until it predicts the sample itself in the last layer. In the original work, the lowest layer is called sample-level layer and the others are called frame-level layers. As we want to control both the content of the speech and the speaker identity, we must somehow inject conditioning information to the system i" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1906_01083_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1906.01083_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1906.01083_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1906_01083_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "and fully endto-end generative model of audio. Our contributions are: We introduce MelNet, a generative model for spectrograms which couples a ne-grained autoregressive model and a multiscale generation procedure to jointly capture local and global structure. We introduce a generative model for audio which captures longer-range dependencies than existing end-to-end models. We primarily achieve this by modelling 2D time-frequency representations such as spectrograms rather than 1D timedomain wave" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1906_01083_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
