    kg:textSpan "the advantages of end-to-end training and softmax pre-training. [TABLE id=1705.02304_p6_t6] caption: Table 6: The system fusion performance of text-independent speaker recognition task using Train50k as train set, Eva200 as test set. columns: system | eer | acc row: system=ResCNN; eer=2.23; acc=90.53 row: system=GRU; eer=2.77; acc=89.50 row: system=embedding fusion; eer=2.17; acc=90.95 row: system=score fusion; eer=2.07; acc=91.83 [/TABLE] [TABLE id=1705.02304_p6_t5] caption: Table 5: The perfor" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1705_09422_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1705.09422_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "1705.09422_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1705_09422_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "The temporal features are overlapping 20ms windows with the stride of 10ms, which are used for the generation of spectrum features. From a 0.8-second sound sample, 80 temporal feature sets (each forms a 40 MFEC features) can be obtained which form the input speech feature map. Each input feature map has the dimensionality of 80 40 which is formed from 80 input frames and their corresponding spectral features, where is the number of utterances used in modeling the speaker during the development a" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1705_09422_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1705.09422_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "1705.09422_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1705_09422_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "of up to 4 sessions of interviews for each of the 1083 different speakers. The WVUMultimodal dataset includes different modalities of data collected over a period from 2013 to 2015. The audio part of data consists of both scripted and unscripted voice samples. For the scripted samples, the participants read a xed sample of text. For the unscripted samples, the participants answer interview questions that require conversational responses. We only use the scripted audio samples, as only the voice " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1705_10874_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1705.10874_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "1705.10874_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1705_10874_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "features (i. e., MFCCs). Specically, the model was trained to predict clean features when presented with a noisy input frame by frame. This enhancement model has been shown to be competitive with other DNN-based mapping models at various levels of SNR when evaluated by ASR systems. Following from this work, Wollmer et al. [69] further proposed to use LSTM-RNNs to handle highly non-stationary additive noise, which was then extended to coping with reverberation in [70][74]. With the help of LSTM-R" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1706_00612_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1706.00612_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "1706.00612_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1706_00612_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "in the center of the activation-valence space, what makes the discrimination from other classes difcult. In contrast, for scripted sessions the accuracy for angry is surprisingly high, and relatively low for sad and happy . In general, there are more errors in almost all classes. One reason for the high discrepancy in the class angry is the different class distribution (many angry samples in scripted sessions). But this does not explain all other differences. The analysis suggests that improvise" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1707_06065v1_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1707.06065v1_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1707.06065v1_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1707_06065v1_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "acoustic variability arising from various factors such as speakers, channel noises, and environments. Unlike other adaptive acoustic models, our proposed approach does not require additional adaptation data or speaker information such as i-vectors. Moreover, the model size is xed as it dynamically generates adaptation parameters. We apply our proposed DLN to deep bidirectional LSTM acoustic models and evaluate them on two benchmark datasets for large vocabulary ASR experiments: WSJ and TED-LIUM " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1707_06588_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1707.06588_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "1707.06588_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1707_06588_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "the output is more directly linked to the inputs, we are able to t new speakers using relatively short audio sequences coupled with automatically generated text. Finally, the output of our system is deterministic, given its input. However, multiple intonations are readily generated by employing priming, which involves initializing the buffer S prior to the synthesis process. Experimentally, we evaluate our method in two ways. For TTS quality, we follow the standard Mean Opinion Score (MOS) exper" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1707_07167_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
